WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:01.829
Speaker 1: Let's get back to the phones.

00:00:01.829 --> 00:00:07.060
We take calls every Friday through the David
Pakman show discord, which you can find at

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:11.800
David Pakman dot com slash discord.

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:15.560
Let's hear from folks like, oh, I don't know.

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:17.990
How about James from Southern California?

00:00:17.990 --> 00:00:19.380
James, welcome.

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:21.679
Speaker 4: Thank you very much.

00:00:21.679 --> 00:00:22.679
Can you hear me?

00:00:22.679 --> 00:00:23.679
Speaker 1: I can hear you fine.

00:00:23.679 --> 00:00:24.679
James.

00:00:24.679 --> 00:00:27.979
Speaker 4: I'm first of all, thank you so
much for everything you've done over the years.

00:00:27.979 --> 00:00:28.979
Thank you.

00:00:28.979 --> 00:00:33.320
I wanted to ask your opinion on something.

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:41.400
What do you think the reality is of us eliminating
the Electoral College system and what would

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:43.750
be the path to that if it is a reality?

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:49.790
Speaker 1: I see it as an almost impossibility
through constitutional amendment.

00:00:49.790 --> 00:00:53.170
This would be one way to change the Electoral
College.

00:00:53.170 --> 00:00:58.990
It would be to actually have a constitutional
convention and change the way that it functions.

00:00:58.990 --> 00:01:01.670
I see that as almost an impossibility.

00:01:01.670 --> 00:01:07.990
The more likely scenario is something called
the National Popular Vote Interstate Compact.

00:01:07.990 --> 00:01:12.390
The way this works is individual states can
design.

00:01:12.390 --> 00:01:18.109
They can decide to assign their electoral
votes however they want.

00:01:18.109 --> 00:01:24.900
Normally what states do is they say whoever
wins our state gets the electoral votes.

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:30.670
But states can say instead we are going to
give our electoral votes to whoever wins the

00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:32.159
national popular vote.

00:01:32.159 --> 00:01:37.320
So you could have a state like, for example,
Pennsylvania, say, in the future we're not

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:41.600
going to pay attention to whether Trump or
Biden win Pennsylvania.

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:44.990
We're going to pay attention to whether Trump
or Biden get more votes nationally.

00:01:44.990 --> 00:01:47.740
And that's who will get Pennsylvania's electoral
votes.

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:50.289
Does that make sense, James, how that would
work?

00:01:50.289 --> 00:01:52.029
Speaker 4: Yes, it does.

00:01:52.029 --> 00:01:56.799
So it sounds like we pretty much if we want
to achieve this, we'd have to start rallying

00:01:56.799 --> 00:01:57.799
on the state level.

00:01:57.799 --> 00:02:02.759
Speaker 1: And the thing is, a bunch of states
have already signed up for this.

00:02:02.759 --> 00:02:09.360
And, of course, for it to go into force, you
need enough states to do it such that their

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:14.530
electoral votes would be more than the 270
that one needs to become president.

00:02:14.530 --> 00:02:19.980
But the problem is we're getting we've got
a lot of the blue states, some red states

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:22.550
or at least purple states are going to need
to come aboard.

00:02:22.550 --> 00:02:24.830
And so that's the that's the challenge right
now.

00:02:24.830 --> 00:02:30.850
But I see that as a way, more likely way to
get to a national popular vote than a constitutional

00:02:30.850 --> 00:02:31.850
amendment.

00:02:31.850 --> 00:02:33.130
Speaker 4: Sounds good.

00:02:33.130 --> 00:02:34.750
At least in some reality.

00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:35.960
They're pulling it off.

00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:36.960
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:37.960
Yeah.

00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:42.300
It's not an impossibility, but it's also,
you know, this is not everyone's top priority,

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:43.580
I think it's fair to say.

00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:44.580
Speaker 4: Yeah.

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:45.580
And that's.

00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:46.580
That's the problem.

00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:47.580
Yeah.

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:48.580
The long shot people aren't pushing for it.

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:51.220
We've got all these important short term things
to deal with.

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:52.220
Speaker 2: Exactly.

00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:53.820
Speaker 4: Just falls by the wayside.

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:55.750
Well, thank you so much for your time, David.

00:02:55.750 --> 00:02:56.750
I appreciate.

00:02:56.750 --> 00:02:57.750
Speaker 1: It.

00:02:57.750 --> 00:02:58.750
All right.

00:02:58.750 --> 00:02:59.750
My pleasure.

00:02:59.750 --> 00:03:03.670
There is James from Southern California making
a number of important declarations.

